1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,767
Lesson 23: K9AY, 4-square for receiving, Hi-Z arrays and the Reception (RDF) page

2
00:00:06,817 --> 00:00:15,134
YA-NEC course, lesson 23: the new receiving antennas of 1.3. Calculators tab, Receiving antennas family.

3
00:00:15,184 --> 00:00:25,696
From 1.3 Low dipole, K9AY, 4-square RX and the Hi-Z arrays are enabled too. The folded rhombic stays grey: it will come with its sources.

4
00:00:25,746 --> 00:00:39,634
K9AY by Gary Breed (QST, September 1997): a vertical loop on the mast, closed on a resistor. Typical values: base 9.2 m, apex 7.6 m, 390 ohm.

5
00:00:39,684 --> 00:00:48,034
Create the NEC model and calculate the RDF: the model opens in the 3D structure, with the lobe and the heights.

6
00:00:48,084 --> 00:00:58,167
Back in the Calculators the figures: RDF 6.8 dB against Hi-Z's 7.2, F/B only 4.3 dB with 390 ohm.

7
00:00:58,217 --> 00:01:09,956
Optimize for F/B: the termination goes up to 1400 ohm, F/B almost 30 dB and RDF 7.8 dB.

8
00:01:10,006 --> 00:01:22,541
Careful: real K9AYs use 390-560 ohm. The model is a single loop closed above the ground: the right resistor is adjusted in the field, listening to the F/B.

9
00:01:22,591 --> 00:01:30,742
4-square for receiving: four short verticals at the corners of a lambda/8 square, firing along the diagonal.

10
00:01:30,792 --> 00:01:42,540
The 3D structure shows the phases of the four sources and the lobe towards the chosen direction.

11
00:01:42,590 --> 00:01:57,672
Optimize the phase (RDF): it tries the phase between rows from 120 to 300 degrees and keeps only lobes within 45 degrees of the direction.

12
00:01:57,722 --> 00:02:09,427
Result: 200 degrees, RDF 12.4 dB, F/B 27.5 dB. An empty phase instead uses the exact rear null, pi + beta times d.

13
00:02:09,477 --> 00:02:22,294
The yellow warnings: verticals on real ground in NEC-2, so the values are indicative; and an RDF above IV3PRK's 9.4 dB, because real phasing networks don't reach the ideal phase.

14
00:02:22,344 --> 00:02:33,028
Hi-Z array: 2 elements in cardioid, 3 in a triangle, 8 in a circle. With 8 elements the phase starts at 220 degrees, which sends the lobe forward.

15
00:02:33,078 --> 00:02:52,955
RDF 9.1 dB against Hi-Z's 13.4: the simple model uses two rows and has no real combiner. The yellow warning says so.

16
00:02:53,005 --> 00:03:07,340
Patterns, Reception (RDF) page: here too the 8 verticals in a circle now have the lobe forward.

17
00:03:07,390 --> 00:03:15,468
Below, the RDF versus frequency and the table of receiving antennas, with the space each one needs.

18
00:03:15,518 --> 00:03:26,947
Sources: K9AY (QST 1997), Hi-Z Antennas, IV3PRK, ON4UN and W8JI. All in the manual, chapter 24.
